<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niger Delta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> Delta is the wide coastal delta of the Niger River in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, a region of creeks, mangroves and fertile soils. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>'s agricultural base in the delta region and surrounding forest produced yams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>. These products supported both local consumption and long-distance trade.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niger-delta.jpg" alt="Niger Delta" width="320" height="328" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigerdelta_NASA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Niger Delta lies directly on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a> and separates the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bight-of-benin">Bight of Benin</a> from the Bight of Bonny. As officially defined by the Nigerian government, it extends over 70,000 square kilometres and makes up 7.5 per cent of the country's land. Historically and on maps it consists of Bayelsa, Delta and Rivers States, but in 2000 the government of Obasanjo added Abia, Akwa Ibom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a>, Edo, Imo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ondo-state">Ondo States</a> to the region.</p><p>It is densely populated, with about 31 million people from over 40 ethnic groups speaking around 250 dialects. The Ijaw are the largest group, present in six states, and the main language groups are the Ijaw languages, Ibibio-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik</a> and the Edoid languages. It was sometimes called the Oil Rivers because it was once a major producer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was the British Oil Rivers Protectorate from 1885 until 1893, when it was expanded as the Niger Coast Protectorate. In the west the British signed separate Treaties of Protection with kings of the four primary groups, the Urhobo, Isoko, Ijaw and Itsekiri. The core of the delta later became part of the eastern region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, created in 1951. In 1953 the non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo peoples</a> of that region, among them the Ibibio, Annang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efik">Efik</a>, Ijaw and Ogoja, demanded a state of their own, the Calabar-Ogoja-Rivers state, which became a major issue in the status of minorities during the debates on independence.</p><p>Isaac Adaka Boro declared an Independent Niger Delta Republic before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a> of 1967 to 1970, in which the people of the delta suffered heavily from loss of life, hunger and starvation, and schools closed. Rivers State, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river-state">Cross River State</a>, Akwa Ibom State and Bayelsa State were created from the old eastern region. After the war communities demanded social and environmental justice, and Ken Saro Wiwa and the Ogoni were the lead figures. The Ogoni Bill of Rights appeared in 1990, and Saro Wiwa and nine other activists were arrested and killed in 1995. In 1998 the Ijaw issued the Kaiama Declaration on control of resources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Oil operations began in the region in the 1950s, run by multinational corporations. Since 1975 the region has accounted for more than 75 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s export earnings. The pollution and gas flaring that came with the industry, together with arguments over the sharing of oil revenue, have fed environmental movements and conflicts in the region. Revenue allocation was contested well before independence, and the derivation formula governs the share that oil-producing states keep.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Niger Delta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fertile forest lands west of the <b>Niger Delta</b> produced yams, palm oil and kola nuts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was governed by the Foreign Office rather than the Colonial Office, and its officials began pushing into the hinterland of the <b>Niger Delta</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
